      "text": "To evaluate \\(\\log_3 (27\\sqrt{3})\\), we will use properties of logarithms and algebraic manipulation to simplify the expression.\n\nFirst, we express the logarithm in terms of base 3:\n\\[\n\\log_3 (27\\sqrt{3})\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms, specifically the product rule:\n\\[\n\\log_b (xy) = \\log_b (x) + \\log_b (y)\n\\]\n\nWe can break down \\(\\log_3 (27\\sqrt{3})\\) into two separate logarithms:\n\\[\n\\log_3 (27\\sqrt{3}) = \\log_3 (27) + \\log_3 (\\sqrt{3})\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify each term individually. We start with \\(\\log_3 (27)\\). We know that 27 can be expressed as a power of 3:\n\\[\n27 = 3^3\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\\[\n\\log_3 (27) = \\log_3 (3^3)\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule:\n\\[\n\\log_b (x^y) = y \\log_b (x)\n\\]\nwe get:\n\\[\n\\log_3 (3^3) = 3 \\log_3 (3)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log_3 (3) = 1\\), we have:\n\\[\n3 \\log_3 (3) = 3 \\cdot 1 = 3\n\\]\n\nNow, we simplify \\(\\log_3 (\\sqrt{3})\\). We recognize that \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) can be written as \\(3^{1/2}\\):\n\\[\n\\log_3 (\\sqrt{3}) = \\log_3 (3^{1/2})\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule again:\n\\[\n\\log_3 (3^{1/2}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\log_3 (3)\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log_3 (3) = 1\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} \\log_3 (3) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow, combining the results of the two logarithms:\n\\[\n\\log_3 (27\\sqrt{3}) = \\log_3 (27) + \\log_3 (\\sqrt{3}) = 3 + \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nTo combine these, we need a common denominator:\n\\[\n3 = \\frac{6}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\n3 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{7}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{2}}\n\\]",
